const HELP_ARTICLES=[
{id:'first-show',category:'Getting started',title:'Create your first fireworks show',summary:'Go from an empty timeline to a saved show in a few steps.',tags:'beginner start quick guide tutorial new',open:'shows',related:['duration','markers','save-project'],body:`<ol><li>Choose a <b>Show duration</b> in the timeline toolbar. Aspect ratio and output resolution are beside it.</li><li>Open <b>Shows</b> and select a show style, or use <b>Generate Random Show</b>. A style fills your chosen duration.</li><li>Press <b>Play</b> under the preview. Pause whenever you want to edit.</li><li>Double-click a timeline diamond to open <b>Marker Management</b>. Adjust its detonation time, shells and positions.</li><li>Choose a background in <b>Scene</b>. Add music through <b>Audio</b> if wanted.</li><li>Use <b>Save Show</b> on the timeline or <b>Save</b> in the top toolbar to save a project. Use <b>Export</b> to create a video.</li></ol><div class="help-note">For a show made entirely by hand, start with an empty timeline and add shells or markers instead of choosing a style.</div>`},
{id:'workspace',category:'Getting started',title:'Find your way around the workspace',summary:'What each area of the app is for.',tags:'layout interface toolbar sidebar panels overview',related:['transport','duration','shell-library'],body:`<table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>Top toolbar</td><td>Project actions, themes, system load, export, Shell Lab, full screen, help and support.</td></tr><tr><td>Shell Library</td><td>Browse designs, preview shells, edit them and place them on the timeline.</td></tr><tr><td>Shows</td><td>Choose generated show styles or reopen a saved project.</td></tr><tr><td>Scene</td><td>Select a background, import custom media and set atmosphere.</td></tr><tr><td>Audio</td><td>Add music, choose the active track, configure BeatSync and control firework sounds.</td></tr><tr><td>Main preview</td><td>Watch the composition using the selected aspect ratio.</td></tr><tr><td>Detonation Timeline</td><td>Arrange when fireworks burst, manage markers, set show/view duration and save the show.</td></tr></table><p>Expandable cards keep each sidebar section compact. Scroll inside a catalog or the sidebar to reach additional content.</p>`},
{id:'save-project',category:'Getting started',title:'Save, reopen and move a show',summary:'Projects retain editable fireworks, music and the selected custom scene.',tags:'save saved shows project file backup open portable audio embedded',open:'shows',related:['export-start','saved-scenes','recovery'],body:`<ol><li>Click <b>Save</b> or <b>Save Show</b>.</li><li>Choose a location and name for the <b>.sfsproject</b> file.</li><li>The saved file is added to <b>Saved Shows</b>. Click its entry to reopen it, or use <b>Open</b> to browse for a project.</li></ol><p>A project stores the editable shell designs, markers, scene and show settings. Loaded playlist files and the selected custom background media are packaged into the project. Including video or multiple songs increases the file size and save time.</p><p>To keep an earlier arrangement, save the new version under another filename. Copy the project file when moving that show to another computer. A video export is a finished movie, not an editable project.</p><div class="help-note">The saved scene catalog belongs to this computer. Saving a project carries its selected scene, not the entire custom scene catalog.</div>`},
{id:'transport',category:'Timeline & shows',title:'Play, pause, loop and preview speed',summary:'Control playback without changing the finished show’s timing.',tags:'1x 2x quarter half speed transport loop stop previous next',related:['duration','fullscreen','audio-tracks'],body:`<table><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Play / Pause</td><td>Start or pause show playback. Space also toggles playback when you are not typing in a field.</td></tr><tr><td>Stop</td><td>Stop playback and return to the beginning.</td></tr><tr><td>Previous / Next</td><td>Move the playhead to a neighboring cue.</td></tr><tr><td>Loop</td><td>Return to the beginning after the show ends. Its highlighted state means looping is on.</td></tr><tr><td>Speed, such as 1×</td><td>Change preview playback speed. The active soundtrack follows the selected preview rate. Export uses normal speed.</td></tr></table><p>The time display shows the current position and total show length. A custom scene video follows show playback and loops as needed.</p>`},
{id:'duration',category:'Timeline & shows',title:'Show duration versus view duration',summary:'Set the length of the show separately from how much timeline you can see.',tags:'duration custom minutes hour length fit zoom scroll dynamic rhythm repeat',related:['show-styles','markers','music-sync'],body:`<p><b>Show duration</b> determines how long the show runs. <b>View duration</b> determines how much time fits across the visible timeline. Choosing a shorter view helps with precise editing; the horizontal scrollbar reaches the rest of the show.</p><p><b>Fit</b> fits the show and active music extent into the visible timeline. The minus and plus controls step through view sizes. Grid spacing changes with the view.</p><h3>Changing the show length</h3><p>Show styles use the duration you select. Extending a show repeats its established burst rhythm to add more cues; shortening uses fewer repetitions. The finale moves toward the new ending, allowing sparks time to fade. It does not slow down particle motion or stretch the music.</p><p>The rhythm is retained when shortening, so lengthening again can reuse the fuller pattern. Cue edits are incorporated when the duration is next changed. Save a separate copy before changing the length of a carefully timed manual or music-synchronized arrangement.</p>`},
{id:'show-styles',category:'Timeline & shows',title:'Use show styles and random shows',summary:'Generate a complete arrangement at your chosen duration.',tags:'curated templates presets gold symphony opening night grand finale generate',open:'shows',related:['duration','markers','save-project'],body:`<p>Show styles such as Opening Night, Gold Symphony and Neon Riot generate arrangements from different combinations of shells, palettes and rhythm. They are starting points you can edit, not locked movies.</p><ol><li>Set <b>Show duration</b>.</li><li>Open <b>Shows</b> and choose a style. If there is already an arrangement, confirm its replacement.</li><li>Preview, then edit individual markers or shell designs.</li></ol><p>Loading a style keeps your music. It replaces the fireworks arrangement and its working shell selection. Use <b>Generate Random Show</b> for a new variation; its intensity slider affects the generated activity.</p><p>Longer shows add more bursts at the established pace. The ending is positioned for the selected duration. Undo can restore the previous arrangement while it remains in the current editing history.</p>`},
{id:'markers',category:'Timeline & shows',title:'Add and move detonation markers',summary:'Diamonds represent burst moments, rather than rocket launch times.',tags:'diamond cue marker drag stuck detonation timeline timing',related:['marker-manager','lanes','duration'],body:`<p>A marker is an exact detonation moment. The app works backward from that moment to schedule each shell’s launch, based on its flight characteristics.</p><ol><li>Move the playhead to the desired time and click <b>Add Marker</b>, or drag a shell tile onto the timeline.</li><li>Drag the marker horizontally to change its time. Separate markers can pass each other without automatically becoming one.</li><li>Double-click its diamond to manage its shells and enter an exact time.</li></ol><p>The number on a marker is its firework count. One marker can contain several shells. An empty marker can be filled later. Placing an aerial detonation extremely close to time zero leaves little or no time for its rocket flight; place it later when you want to see the full launch.</p>`},
{id:'marker-manager',category:'Timeline & shows',title:'Arrange shells inside a marker',summary:'Choose shells, control their positions and edit a shared burst moment.',tags:'marker management add shell chooser duplicate horizontal position height',related:['markers','shell-lab','lanes'],body:`<ol><li>Double-click a marker.</li><li>Enter its <b>Detonation time</b> for the whole group.</li><li>Click <b>Add Shell</b> to open the shell chooser. Select a tile, inspect its small preview, then add it.</li><li>Use each row’s position slider to place that firework across the stage. Its lane selector organizes it in the timeline.</li><li>Use <b>Edit</b> to open its design in Shell Lab, <b>Dup</b> to duplicate a row, or <b>Del</b> to remove it.</li></ol><p>Duplicating a marker copies the group. The manager’s Delete All removes the marker and its fireworks. Close the manager when finished; it is not a separate project save operation.</p><div class="help-note">Several timeline uses can share one shell design. Saving an edit to that design changes its other uses too.</div>`},
{id:'lanes',category:'Timeline & shows',title:'Understand lanes and clear controls',summary:'Keep high, low and ground effects organized without losing your music.',tags:'high aerial low mid ground lane clear all delete collapse',related:['markers','audio-tracks','undo'],body:`<p>The fireworks lanes are <b>High Aerial</b>, <b>Low / Mid Aerial</b> and <b>Ground</b>. The music lane displays the active soundtrack. Lane assignment organizes cues; a shell’s actual flight and burst height are controlled by its design.</p><p><b>Collapse Lanes</b> combines the firework display into one lane. Expand it to separate the categories again. This is a display choice, not a change to the arrangement.</p><p><b>Clear All</b> on the timeline clears fireworks and markers after confirmation. It keeps music and scene settings. The playlist has its own <b>Clear All</b>, which removes music tracks.</p><p>Starting a completely <b>New</b> project is different: it replaces the current project, including loaded music. Save first if you want to keep that show.</p>`},
{id:'shell-library',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Browse, preview and add shells',summary:'Find a design and bring it into the show or Shell Lab.',tags:'shell family filter search tiles thumbnail library plus',open:'shells',related:['shell-lab','rain','shapes'],body:`<p>Use the shell search box for a name or palette. The family dropdown narrows the catalog to categories such as Cascading or geometric patterns.</p><p>Click a tile to preview its shell. Use its corner controls to open it in <b>Shell Lab</b> or add it to the timeline at the playhead. You can also drag the tile onto the timeline. A number on the tile shows uses of that working shell in the show.</p><p>Thumbnails are generated from the shell design, including its colors and effect settings. They update as the design changes. A thumbnail is a single moment of an animated effect; preview playback shows its launch, evolution and fall.</p><p>Custom working shells appear alongside the catalog entries. Save the project to keep your design work.</p>`},
{id:'shell-lab',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Design and save a shell',summary:'Use the library, tool cards, preview and settings together.',tags:'shell lab save shell new reset defaults randomize preview drawing cards draft',related:['design-controls','physics-controls','effects-controls','layers'],body:`<ol><li>Open <b>Shell Lab</b> from a shell tile or the top toolbar.</li><li>Select a shell from the library column on the far left. The separate tools column immediately beside it contains Undo/Redo, drawing, shape management, correction, comparison and shell actions. The preview/edit canvas is next, with property settings on its right. Its name appears in the window header, and selecting it automatically plays its launch and burst from the beginning. Pause/Resume and the preview time slider let you inspect a particular moment.</li><li>Change settings in <b>Design</b>, <b>Physics</b>, <b>Effects</b> or <b>Layers &amp; Detail</b>. Shape adds the drawing controls, Burst Steps arranges timed accents, Changes Over Time programs colors and movement, and Saved Designs stores reusable work.</li><li>Click <b>Preview</b>, immediately left of Pause/Resume at the bottom, to restart and play the current draft in the center preview.</li><li>Click <b>Save Shell</b> to apply the draft to its working design. Then save the project to keep it on disk.</li></ol><p><b>New Shell</b> starts a custom design. <b>Reset Defaults</b> restores the selected design’s defaults. <b>Randomize</b> creates a variation to explore.</p><div class="help-note">Switching shell designs can apply new type defaults. Make fine adjustments after choosing the type. Drafts are kept while switching inside the current editing session, but are not a substitute for Save Shell and saving the project.</div>`},
{id:'design-controls',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Design controls: identity, geometry and color',summary:'Shape the burst and its palette before refining motion.',tags:'size density spread rotation tilt irregularity colors color program age wave random breaks',related:['color-programs','physics-controls','shapes'],body:`<table><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Name / shell design / family</td><td>Name the working shell, choose its base design and classify it for library filtering.</td></tr><tr><td>Palette / color swatches</td><td>Choose a color set, then edit individual palette colors.</td></tr><tr><td>Breaks / break interval</td><td>Create repeated breaks from one shell. The interval control appears when more than one break is selected.</td></tr><tr><td>Shell size</td><td>Changes the scale and amount of the burst.</td></tr><tr><td>Particle density</td><td>Changes the number of emitted stars; higher values cost more processing.</td></tr><tr><td>Burst spread</td><td>Controls how widely the pattern expands.</td></tr><tr><td>Physical irregularity</td><td>Adds directional randomness. Keep it low for recognizable symbols.</td></tr><tr><td>Rotation / depth and tilt</td><td>Orient or flatten supported pattern geometry. Their visible effect depends on the chosen design.</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'color-programs',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Choose a color program',summary:'Control how stars use and change palette colors.',tags:'sequence age wave random color change point palette transition',related:['design-controls','materials'],body:`<table><tr><th>Program</th><th>Behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Sequence</td><td>Distributes the palette colors across the emitted stars in order.</td></tr><tr><td>Age</td><td>Each star changes to its next palette color after a fraction of its lifetime.</td></tr><tr><td>Wave</td><td>A color change travels across the particle order as the burst evolves.</td></tr><tr><td>Random</td><td>Selects palette colors using the shell’s seeded generator.</td></tr></table><p><b>Color change point</b> appears in Age mode. A value of 0.5 means halfway through a particle’s life, not half a second. Lifetime variation means particles can change at slightly different moments.</p><p>For a clear transformation, choose noticeably different neighboring swatches. Similar shades produce a subtler transition. Selecting another palette replaces the current swatch colors.</p>`},
{id:'physics-controls',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Physics controls: flight and falling trails',summary:'Tune launch height, expansion, gravity and persistence.',tags:'launch angle height speed wobble gravity drag wind lifetime trails physics fall',related:['rain','design-controls','effects-controls'],body:`<table><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>Launch height / speed / angle</td><td>Control the rocket’s flight toward its burst point.</td></tr><tr><td>Position variation</td><td>Add small differences to the horizontal launch origin.</td></tr><tr><td>Particle speed / speed variance</td><td>Set outward motion and how much individual speeds differ.</td></tr><tr><td>Gravity</td><td>Increase downward acceleration for a heavier fall.</td></tr><tr><td>Air drag</td><td>Reduce motion more quickly as stars travel.</td></tr><tr><td>Wind response</td><td>Set sensitivity to the scene wind. Zero ignores wind.</td></tr><tr><td>Lifetime / lifetime variance</td><td>Control how long stars remain and how unevenly they disappear.</td></tr><tr><td>Trail length / width</td><td>Control the visible streak behind each moving star.</td></tr></table><p>Scene gravity and wind also affect the full show. Adjust one group at a time and preview so you can see which setting caused a change.</p>`},
{id:'effects-controls',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Effects controls: glow, sparkle and fragments',summary:'Give a shell its texture without losing its underlying shape.',tags:'bloom flicker glitter strobe crackle crossette micro stars smoke',related:['materials','advanced-controls','sound'],body:`<table><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Bloom</td><td>Glow around the stars; high values can make nearby stars merge visually.</td></tr><tr><td>Flicker</td><td>Small brightness changes while stars burn.</td></tr><tr><td>Glitter</td><td>Bright sparkles during the effect.</td></tr><tr><td>Strobe</td><td>Distinct pulsing flashes.</td></tr><tr><td>Crackle</td><td>Fine fragment sparks near the later part of a star’s life.</td></tr><tr><td>Crossette splits</td><td>Split stars into crossing branches; this can be added to other shell designs.</td></tr><tr><td>Micro stars</td><td>Additional small secondary particles.</td></tr><tr><td>Smoke / smoke lifetime</td><td>Set the plume strength and persistence. Scene smoke density also affects visibility.</td></tr></table><p>Strong fragmentation increases particle load. If a shape becomes hard to recognize, reduce secondary effects and bloom before increasing density.</p>`},
{id:'layers',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Build a shell with multiple layers',summary:'Combine up to twelve independently timed patterns in one aerial shell.',tags:'advanced layered burst layer stack pistil core crown add duplicate solo delay',related:['layer-controls','materials','advanced-controls'],body:`<ol><li>Open <b>Layers &amp; Detail</b> and enable <b>Layered burst engine</b>.</li><li>Select a layer in the stack. The initial stack supplies an outer crown, inner pistil and core.</li><li>Choose each layer’s pattern, palette and material, then adjust its relative size and lifetime.</li><li>Use <b>Start delay (s)</b> to separate layers in time. A small delay can let a bright center appear just after the outer crown.</li><li>Use <b>S</b> to preview a layer alone; click it again to leave solo preview.</li></ol><p>Add and Duplicate stop at twelve layers. Up and down reorder the stack; Delete removes a layer while retaining at least one. Keep at least one layer enabled: an all-disabled stack currently falls back to the master burst.</p><div class="help-note">Layered bursts are an aerial capability. Fountain and mine use a separate ground-effect path.</div>`},
{id:'layer-controls',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Layer controls and master inheritance',summary:'Understand scales, added effects and the layer palette.',tags:'inherit master layer density size speed spread lifetime rotation trail material',related:['layers','materials','color-programs'],body:`<p>Layer scale controls multiply the master shell’s values. For example, a size scale of 0.5 creates a smaller component inside the main burst; a lifetime scale below 1 fades that layer sooner.</p><table><tr><th>Layer control</th><th>Behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Pattern / palette / colors</td><td>Choose geometry and colors for that layer.</td></tr><tr><td>Material</td><td>Use Inherit master to follow the master material, or choose a specific material.</td></tr><tr><td>Size / density / expansion / spread</td><td>Scale the component’s amount and outward motion.</td></tr><tr><td>Lifetime / trail scales</td><td>Change persistence and streak proportions relative to the master.</td></tr><tr><td>Rotation</td><td>Add an angle to supported master pattern rotation.</td></tr><tr><td>Ignition delay</td><td>Wait this many seconds after the burst before emitting the layer.</td></tr><tr><td>Added glitter / crackle / strobe</td><td>Add to the master effect strengths, up to their limits.</td></tr></table>`},
{id:'materials',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Choose a star material',summary:'Materials adjust how particles burn and move.',tags:'pearl streamer glitter ember strobe crackle material',related:['layers','effects-controls'],body:`<table><tr><th>Material</th><th>Character</th></tr><tr><td>Pearl stars</td><td>A neutral starting point that leaves the underlying controls largely as set.</td></tr><tr><td>Long streamers</td><td>Longer trails and lifetime, with reduced drag.</td></tr><tr><td>Glitter compound</td><td>Strong sparkle, flicker and small secondary stars.</td></tr><tr><td>Heavy embers</td><td>Heavier downward motion, reduced glow and shorter trails.</td></tr><tr><td>Strobe stars</td><td>A strongly pulsing appearance.</td></tr><tr><td>Crackle stars</td><td>Strong fragmentation with a shorter burn.</td></tr></table><p>Materials are behavior presets applied on top of shell settings. A selected material can enforce a stronger effect than an individual low slider value suggests. Choose Pearl when you want the most direct control of those settings.</p>`},
{id:'advanced-controls',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Advanced volume, movement and fading',summary:'Refine the shape and burn after the main design is working.',tags:'volume sphere disc dome cone cylinder hourglass thickness radial energy turbulence decay taper onset frequency performance',related:['layers','materials','performance'],body:`<table><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>Burst profile</td><td>Reshape directions into a sphere, compressed disc, dome, cone, hourglass or cylinder.</td></tr><tr><td>Burst thickness</td><td>Adjust thickness or spread within supported volume modes. A sphere with zero radial bias bypasses this adjustment.</td></tr><tr><td>Inner / outer expansion</td><td>Redistribute expansion strength across the pattern.</td></tr><tr><td>Small movement variation</td><td>Add small disturbances to particle motion.</td></tr><tr><td>Brightness fade</td><td>Lower values retain brightness longer; higher values fade sooner.</td></tr><tr><td>Trail fade</td><td>Control how trails shrink over the particle’s life.</td></tr><tr><td>Sparkle start time</td><td>Delay sparkle until a fraction of the particle’s life.</td></tr><tr><td>Flash rate</td><td>Change the pulse rate when strobe is active.</td></tr></table><p>Particle, lifetime and load readouts in Shell Lab are estimates for the design. They are separate from the system-wide hardware meter.</p>`},
{id:'rain',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Make a shower of falling sparks',summary:'Start with Golden Rain or Silver Rain for a strong cascading effect.',tags:'rain waterfall shower sparks golden silver willow horsetail kamuro cascading',open:'shells',related:['physics-controls','advanced-controls'],body:`<ol><li>Open Shell Library, choose <b>Cascading</b>, or search for <b>rain</b>.</li><li>Try <b>Golden Rain</b> for dense warm trails or <b>Silver Rain</b> for a broader bright shower.</li><li>Open the shell in Shell Lab. Increase lifetime and trail length for persistence; tune gravity and drag for the fall.</li><li>Use a lower luminous decay curve in Advanced to keep the later fall visible.</li><li>Place it high enough and leave time after the last burst for the sparks to finish falling.</li></ol><p>Waterfall, Horsetail, Willow and Kamuro offer other cascading starting points. These release a burst whose stars then descend. They do not continuously create a new stream of sparks for an arbitrary duration.</p>`},
{id:'shapes',category:'Shell Lab',title:'Keep hearts, rings and symbols recognizable',summary:'Protect the geometry when building pattern shells.',tags:'heart star ring butterfly smiley crown shape geometry thumbnail',related:['design-controls','effects-controls','layers'],body:`<p>Choose the pattern first, then refine its controls. Low physical irregularity and low speed variance keep the stars near the intended outline. Excessive spread, fragmentation or bloom can make a symbol look like a general burst.</p><p>Preview it over time: some patterns read best shortly after detonation, then distort naturally as gravity and wind take effect. Rotation and tilt are implemented differently between patterns and are not equally visible on every design.</p><p>For a layered symbol, keep the outline layer clear and give its center a smaller, shorter-lived accent. Use Solo to check the outline without the other layers. Thumbnails update from the working design but show only one sampled moment.</p>`},
{id:'audio-tracks',category:'Audio',title:'Add music and choose the active track',summary:'Import your own audio or use the built-in sample collection.',tags:'playlist sample music song add browse drag drop active track waveform',open:'audio',related:['music-sync','sound','save-project'],body:`<ol><li>Open <b>Audio → Music</b>. Drop audio files into the music area or browse for them.</li><li>For bundled music, choose a sample track and click its Add button.</li><li>Expand <b>Playlist</b>. Use a row’s preview button to listen; use <b>+</b> to make that track active on the show timeline.</li></ol><p>One track is active for synchronized playback and export at a time. Other tracks remain in the playlist; they do not automatically play one after another. The waveform appears only for active, analyzed audio and follows the actual track length.</p><p>Remove an individual row with its remove button, or use the playlist’s Clear All. Timeline Clear All keeps music. Saving the project packages the loaded playlist files.</p>`},
{id:'music-sync',category:'Audio',title:'Build fireworks from music with BeatSync',summary:'Opt into editable choreography based on the active track’s beat analysis.',tags:'beatsync beat sync music rhythm intensity strictness complexity seed automatic',open:'audio',related:['audio-tracks','duration','markers'],body:`<p><b>Build show from music</b> is optional and off by default. Leave it off when you only want a soundtrack. Enable it to let BeatSync generate an editable arrangement from the active track after analysis.</p><table><tr><th>Control</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Intensity</td><td>Controls overall generated activity.</td></tr><tr><td>Sync strictness</td><td>Changes how closely the arrangement follows analyzed beat information.</td></tr><tr><td>Complexity</td><td>Changes the variety and layering of the generated arrangement.</td></tr><tr><td>Seed</td><td>Selects a repeatable generated variation.</td></tr></table><p>BeatSync uses the analyzed track duration. Its generated markers can then be edited manually. Changing show duration later repeats the show rhythm; it does not stretch the song or create audio after the track ends. Keep the music-based duration when exact track alignment matters.</p>`},
{id:'sound',category:'Audio',title:'Control launch, burst, crackle and whistle sounds',summary:'Separate global sound choices from each shell’s audio settings.',tags:'sound effects sfx whistle no sound launch burst crackle volume mute',open:'audio',related:['effects-controls','export-audio','troubleshoot-audio'],body:`<p>In <b>Audio → Firework Sound Effects</b>, independently enable <b>Launch</b>, <b>Burst</b>, <b>Crackle</b> and <b>Whistle</b>. The volume slider controls the overall effect level.</p><p>Shell Lab also has a per-shell launch/burst/crackle switch, a launch whistle switch and an effect volume setting. The global switches and the selected shell’s settings together determine what you hear. Firework loudness also follows shell size.</p><p>Visual crackle and audible crackle are different controls: a shell can have a sparkling appearance while that sound category is muted. Imported scene videos are always muted. Music comes from the active playlist track.</p>`},
{id:'scene-catalog',category:'Scenes',title:'Choose and manage scene backgrounds',summary:'Use the six-tile scrolling catalog for both built-in and saved custom scenes.',tags:'scene background catalog six 6 thumbnails delete remove image landscape',open:'scene',related:['custom-scene','atmosphere','saved-scenes'],body:`<p>Expand <b>Scene Background</b> to choose an environment. The catalog shows up to six tiles at once; scroll inside it for more. Titles sit above the preview so the landscape remains visible.</p><p>Saved custom images and videos appear in this same catalog. The separate Custom card is for importing and saving, not storing thumbnails.</p><p>Where available, the minus corner deletes a scene after confirmation. Removing a built-in image removes its catalog entry on this computer. Deleting a saved custom scene removes its catalog media; it does not delete your original source file or previously saved project files.</p><p>Deep Night and Solid Black are useful clean starting points. Stars and haze are controlled separately.</p>`},
{id:'custom-scene',category:'Scenes',title:'Import and save an image or video scene',summary:'Turn your own media into a reusable catalog entry.',tags:'custom scene save image video drag drop browse mp4 webm png jpg import',open:'scene',related:['scene-video','saved-scenes','scene-catalog'],body:`<ol><li>Open <b>Scene → Custom</b>.</li><li>Drop one image or video into the import area, or click to browse.</li><li>Wait for the file to be checked, then give the scene a name.</li><li>Click <b>Save Scene</b>. The new scene appears in Scene Background and is selected for the show.</li></ol><p>Images can be PNG, JPG, WebP or BMP. Videos can be MP4 or WebM with a codec supported by the installed runtime. Files must be smaller than 256 MB. An unsupported file produces a message rather than adding a broken tile.</p><p>The Custom card displays the staged filename and save controls. Its media is not added to the catalog until Save Scene succeeds.</p>`},
{id:'scene-video',category:'Scenes',title:'Use video as a background',summary:'Understand looping, playback, framing and video export.',tags:'video background loop mute pause codec mp4 webm motion',related:['custom-scene','export-start','troubleshoot-scenes'],body:`<p>A selected custom video follows the show’s playhead, pauses with playback and loops when the show outlasts the clip. It is muted so it does not compete with the playlist or firework effects.</p><p>The video is scaled to cover the stage. A source with a different aspect ratio can be cropped at its edges. Choose media with room for the important landscape features and use the intended output aspect ratio before judging the framing.</p><p>Video frames are included in the final rendered show. The selected video is also packaged when saving a project. High-resolution or long clips increase decoding work, save size and export time.</p><p>A filename ending in MP4 does not guarantee a supported codec. If a clip cannot load, try a conventional H.264 MP4 or a WebM file that plays in your installed browser runtime.</p>`},
{id:'atmosphere',category:'Scenes',title:'Stars, haze and global show settings',summary:'Set the environment and overall appearance of the composition.',tags:'stars density haze wind gravity smoke bloom exposure shake global size intensity',open:'scene',related:['physics-controls','performance','troubleshoot-scenes'],body:`<table><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr><tr><td>Show stars / star density</td><td>Toggle and adjust the app’s added star field.</td></tr><tr><td>Atmospheric haze</td><td>Add a low-sky atmospheric glow.</td></tr><tr><td>Global intensity / size</td><td>Scale overall firework density and size during show playback.</td></tr><tr><td>Gravity / wind</td><td>Set scene-wide forces; each shell also has its own response.</td></tr><tr><td>Smoke density</td><td>Adjust visibility of the smoke emitted by shells.</td></tr><tr><td>Bloom / exposure</td><td>Control overall glow and brightness.</td></tr><tr><td>Camera shake</td><td>Add movement around stronger bursts.</td></tr></table><p>The star checkbox only controls generated stars. Stars photographed or rendered into an imported image or video remain part of that media. Built-in photographic scenes were prepared without stars so the checkbox can control the added field.</p>`},
{id:'saved-scenes',category:'Scenes',title:'Where saved scenes and project media live',summary:'Know what travels with a project and what stays in the local catalog.',tags:'save scene storage appdata portable bundled media custom files move backup',related:['save-project','custom-scene','requirements'],body:`<p>Saved custom scenes are stored in Fireworks Premier’s normal local application-data folder. They remain in the catalog after closing and reopening the app. You do not need companion folders beside the EXE.</p><p>When a custom scene is selected and you save a project, that scene’s media is embedded in the project. This allows the saved show to carry its background to another computer. It does not copy every scene from the catalog.</p><p>Keep your original source media and saved projects if you want independent backups. Clearing the app’s application-data folder also removes locally saved custom scenes. Removing a scene from the catalog does not reach into previously saved project files.</p>`},
{id:'export-start',category:'Export & output',title:'Export a finished show',summary:'Create a video, animated GIF or PNG sequence from your project.',tags:'export render webm vp8 opus gif png sequence movie video save',related:['output-settings','export-audio','export-time'],body:`<ol><li>Save your editable project first.</li><li>Click <b>Export</b> in the top toolbar.</li><li>Choose format, resolution, frame rate, duration, quality and audio.</li><li>Click <b>Start Export</b> and choose a destination.</li><li>Wait through rendering and encoding. Use the export cancel control if you need to stop.</li></ol><p>WebM uses VP8 video and Opus audio; MP4 export is not included. GIF is useful for a short animation without sound. PNG sequence creates a ZIP of rendered image frames. Choose Full Show for the whole project or a short export duration for a sample.</p><div class="help-note">Export creates finished media. Keep the .sfsproject file for later editing.</div>`},
{id:'output-settings',category:'Export & output',title:'Aspect ratio, resolution and frame rate',summary:'Choose the shape and size of the finished picture.',tags:'aspect ratio resolution 4k vertical square ultrawide fps 30 60 quality',related:['export-start','scene-video','performance'],body:`<p><b>Aspect ratio</b> is the shape of the picture: widescreen, vertical, square and other options. <b>Resolution</b> is its pixel size. The main preview fits that shape inside the available stage area; unused space around it does not become part of the picture.</p><p>The export dialog has its own resolution, frame-rate and quality choices. Check those before starting a final export. Higher resolution and higher particle quality require more processing and produce larger output.</p><p>Frame rate controls frames per second in the export. The app limits 4K-class exports to 30 FPS for reliability. Ultra and Cinema increase particle detail. Cinema is not currently a faster-than-real-time offline renderer.</p>`},
{id:'export-audio',category:'Export & output',title:'What audio is included in an export?',summary:'Use the active music track and enabled firework effects.',tags:'export music soundtrack audio include mute silent sound gif png',related:['audio-tracks','sound','export-start'],body:`<p>Choose <b>Include loaded music</b> to capture the active playlist track and enabled firework sounds. The playlist may contain several files, but only one is active on the timeline. Other rows are not automatically sequenced.</p><p>Choose <b>No audio</b> for silent output. GIF and PNG sequences do not carry audio. Custom scene videos are muted even when export audio is enabled.</p><p>The export runs at normal speed regardless of the preview speed dropdown. A soundtrack shorter than the show does not automatically grow to match it. Check the music lane’s length before exporting a long show.</p>`},
{id:'export-time',category:'Export & output',title:'Why rendering can take a long time',summary:'Rendering and encoding are separate stages of export.',tags:'slow export rendering encoding cinema offline cancel progress',related:['performance','output-settings','troubleshoot-export'],body:`<p>The current exporter captures a fixed-step show while pacing frames in real time, then encodes the result. A long show can therefore take at least roughly its playback duration to capture, with additional time for encoding. Heavy effects or video backgrounds can take longer.</p><p>For a quick check, export a short segment at a smaller resolution. For a final file, choose the intended full duration and resolution. Avoid assuming Cinema will finish faster; it increases rendering detail.</p><p>Wait for the completion message rather than treating the first progress bar change as a finished file. Cancellation can leave no completed output, so keep the editable project separately.</p>`},
{id:'performance',category:'Reference',title:'Read the System Load meter',summary:'Understand the whole-computer meter and the separate shell estimate.',tags:'system load cpu gpu ram memory percent idle heavy particles performance',related:['advanced-controls','output-settings','troubleshoot-performance'],body:`<p>The top meter reports the <b>whole computer</b>, including other apps. CPU is processor use, GPU is the busiest measured graphics engine, and RAM is physical memory in use compared with total memory. The main percentage follows the highest available resource percentage.</p><p>A high reading while Fireworks Premier is idle can come from memory already used by Windows and other programs. It does not mean this app is using that percentage of the CPU. A dash means a reading is unavailable.</p><p>Shell Lab’s particle, lifetime and LIGHT / BALANCED / HEAVY readouts estimate a shell design. They do not measure live hardware use and do not include every possible overlap in the full show.</p>`},
{id:'themes',category:'Reference',title:'Choose a visual theme',summary:'Change the editor’s colors without changing the rendered show.',tags:'theme gunmetal forest ember midnight amethyst aurora sandstone colors contrast',related:['workspace','output-settings'],body:`<p>Use the theme dropdown in the top toolbar. Gunmetal, Forest, Ember, Midnight, Amethyst, Aurora and Sandstone change the editor’s surfaces, borders, text and accents.</p><p>Midnight uses navy and ice blue, Amethyst uses smoky violet and lavender, and Aurora uses deep teal and seafoam. Ivory has been removed.</p><p>The dropdown initially shows <b>Theme</b> until you choose an option in the session, while the editor can still restore your saved appearance. Theme choice affects the interface, not the colors of your fireworks, scene media or exported picture.</p>`},
{id:'fullscreen',category:'Reference',title:'Use full-screen preview',summary:'Present the show with the editing panels hidden.',tags:'fullscreen full screen F11 presentation escape exit preview',related:['transport','output-settings'],body:`<p>Click <b>Full Screen</b> or press <b>F11</b> to enter presentation preview. The editor panels and timeline are hidden so the stage can use the window.</p><p>Use the on-screen exit control, F11, or Escape to leave full-screen preview. This is a viewing mode; it does not export a movie or change your project’s aspect ratio.</p><p>Set up playback and looping before entering if you want to present a repeating show.</p>`},
{id:'undo',category:'Reference',title:'Undo, redo and keyboard shortcuts',summary:'Recover recent edits and use the main keyboard controls.',tags:'undo redo keyboard shortcuts ctrl s z y delete space f1 escape',related:['save-project','recovery'],body:`<table><tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Action</th></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+S</td><td>Save the project.</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y</td><td>Undo / redo recent recorded edits.</td></tr><tr><td>Space</td><td>Toggle show playback when not typing in a field.</td></tr><tr><td>Delete</td><td>Remove the selected timeline event when the editing context allows it.</td></tr><tr><td>F11</td><td>Toggle full-screen preview.</td></tr><tr><td>F1</td><td>Open Help Center.</td></tr><tr><td>Escape</td><td>Close help/support dialogs or leave full-screen preview.</td></tr></table><p>Undo history is limited to recent editing snapshots and is not a disk backup. Some controls record changes at different points, so save project versions before major revisions. Catalog deletions are permanent local library actions and should not be treated as ordinary timeline undo.</p>`},
{id:'requirements',category:'Reference',title:'Portable EXE, requirements and local data',summary:'What is bundled and what the computer still needs.',tags:'install installer dependencies webview2 dotnet ffmpeg standalone portable exe md',related:['save-project','saved-scenes','recovery'],body:`<p>The portable EXE bundles the application, .NET runtime, sample music, scene assets, icons and a restricted FFmpeg build. Open-source licenses and the corresponding component source are embedded too: use the two buttons at the bottom of Help Center to view licenses or save source. No separate download, ZIP distribution or companion asset folder is required.</p><p>The computer still needs <b>Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime</b>, which supplies the embedded browser used by the interface. A single EXE does not mean the app has no operating-system or runtime requirements.</p><p>The app uses its normal local application-data folder for preferences, custom scenes and extracted resources. User projects are saved where you choose. Keep those projects and local custom scene data when cleaning up old builds.</p>`},
{id:'troubleshoot-audio',category:'Troubleshooting',title:'Music or firework sound is missing',summary:'Check the active track and the relevant sound controls.',tags:'silent no audio mute whistle music not playing soundtrack',open:'audio',related:['audio-tracks','sound','export-audio'],body:`<ol><li>In Playlist, use <b>+</b> to put the intended track on the timeline. Previewing a row is not the same as choosing the active track.</li><li>Check the track and app volume, Windows volume, and the selected output device.</li><li>For firework sounds, check the Launch, Burst, Crackle and Whistle categories and effect volume.</li><li>Check the selected shell’s sound, whistle and volume settings in Shell Lab.</li><li>For exported files, check that audio was enabled and that the chosen output format supports sound.</li></ol><p>Imported scene videos are intentionally muted. If the music ends before the show, the remaining portion has no soundtrack unless you use a longer active track.</p>`},
{id:'troubleshoot-scenes',category:'Troubleshooting',title:'A scene is missing, cropped or still shows stars',summary:'Separate media problems from atmosphere settings.',tags:'background video image cropped black stars missing scene unsupported',open:'scene',related:['custom-scene','scene-video','atmosphere'],body:`<p><b>No new catalog tile:</b> importing stages a file; click Save Scene to add it. Read the message in Custom if decoding or saving failed.</p><p><b>Black or unsupported video:</b> try another MP4/WebM encoding. A supported container name can still hold an unsupported codec. Allow large clips time to load.</p><p><b>Cropped landscape:</b> the main scene covers the chosen aspect ratio. Use a matching source shape or leave extra space around important features.</p><p><b>Stars remain after Show Stars is off:</b> stars already present in imported media are part of that image or video. The checkbox only controls the app’s added star field.</p><p><b>Scene missing after moving computers:</b> open a project saved with that custom scene selected, or import and save its original media on the new computer.</p>`},
{id:'troubleshoot-export',category:'Troubleshooting',title:'Export is slow, fails or has the wrong length',summary:'Check duration, destination and rendering workload.',tags:'export error fails duration slow wrong length resolution disk',related:['export-time','export-start','duration'],body:`<ol><li>Check <b>Show duration</b> and the export dialog’s duration choice. A short sample export is not the full show.</li><li>Choose a writable destination with enough free disk space.</li><li>Try a short export at lower resolution and quality to narrow down a workload problem.</li><li>If using a custom video, confirm that it loads and plays in the show.</li><li>Wait for both rendering and encoding to finish.</li></ol><p>The current exporter is paced in real time; Cinema does not remove that limit. If a reproducible problem remains, contact support with the version, export choices and exact error text. Include a small project or sample only if you choose to attach it.</p>`},
{id:'troubleshoot-performance',category:'Troubleshooting',title:'Preview is crowded or running slowly',summary:'Reduce overlapping work while preserving the show’s character.',tags:'lag slow particles heavy performance fps crowded glow stutter',related:['performance','effects-controls','advanced-controls'],body:`<p>Look for many dense shells detonating together, multiple active layers, strong crackle/crossette effects, and high-resolution video backgrounds. These can multiply the work visible in a single frame.</p><p>Reduce particle density or simultaneous launches first. Lower global bloom if stars blend into a bright mass, and reduce smoke density if the scene looks obscured. A smaller output or a less demanding video background can help when decoding and rendering compete.</p><p>Use the System Load readouts to distinguish CPU, GPU and memory pressure. The meter includes other programs, so close unnecessary heavy applications if appropriate. The Lab load label is a design estimate, not a guarantee of smooth playback for an entire show.</p>`},
{id:'contact',category:'Troubleshooting',title:'Contact ShamonSoft support',summary:'Copy the support address or open a draft in an available mail app.',tags:'support contact email outlook thunderbird copy address help bug report',related:['requirements','troubleshoot-export'],body:`<p>Click <b>Contact Support</b> in the top toolbar or at the bottom of this Help Center. The address is <b>support@shamonsoft.com</b>.</p><p><b>Copy Address</b> puts it on your clipboard. <b>Default email app</b> asks Windows to open a new draft. Additional email options are shown when Windows has registered their mail-link support, including Outlook when available. The list can also include a browser registered for webmail.</p><p>If an app cannot open, copy the address into the mail service you normally use. Include the Fireworks Premier version, your steps, expected result and actual result. Nothing is sent automatically, and no project or diagnostic file is attached without your action.</p>`},
{id:'recovery',category:'Getting started',title:'Recover an unsaved show',summary:'Restore the show and its playlist after an interrupted session.',tags:'autosave recovery crash music files',related:['save-project','audio-tracks'],body:`<p>Unsaved changes are automatically checkpointed with the playlist audio. At the next launch, choose whether to restore the autosaved show. Restored unsaved changes still need an ordinary project save.</p><p>The footer reports recovery status. If recovery storage is unavailable, save the project yourself. Recovery is kept in this Windows profile; it is not a substitute for saved projects and backups. Older autosaves may lack audio: the app warns before opening them without music.</p>`},
{id:'safe-save-open',category:'Getting started',title:'Save safely and open another project',summary:'Keep edits and media intact when saving or changing shows.',tags:'save as cancel invalid corrupt missing music',related:['save-project','recovery','audio-tracks'],body:`<p><b>Save</b> updates the current file. <b>Save As</b> (Ctrl+Shift+S) chooses another filename or location. Music and the active custom scene travel with the saved project.</p><p>You can continue editing while media is packaged. Edits made after a save starts remain unsaved and the app warns before closing. Opening another file offers Save First, Discard Changes or Cancel. A malformed project or unreadable embedded track leaves the current show intact.</p><p>Project and export files are written to a temporary file beside the destination, then replace the destination only after writing succeeds. Keep enough free space for both versions until that operation completes.</p>`}
];
HELP_ARTICLES.push(...[{"id": "lab-drawing", "category": "Shell Lab", "title": "Draw shapes with automatic correction", "summary": "Use freehand smoothing, editable curves and simple shapes.", "tags": "canvas bezier freehand curve drawing svg text shapes cutout mirror select points smooth smoothing correction round corners reduce points auto freehand undo redo shape manager list add remove delete clear all duplicate rename empty", "related": ["lab-geometry", "lab-preview", "lab-assets"], "body": "<p>The <b>Shapes in This Design</b> card in the tools column between the library and canvas lists each shape with a thumbnail, name, point count and its own <b>Remove</b> button. Click a thumbnail or point-count row to select a shape and open its drawing canvas. Rename shapes directly in the list. Choose Oval, Box, Star, Line, Freehand, Curve or Individual Stars, then click <b>Add Shape</b>. Freehand adds a new shape when you draw; Curve and Individual Stars start with an empty shape for placing points.</p><p><b>Duplicate Selected</b> makes a copy. <b>Remove</b> removes just that row; <b>Clear All Shapes</b> leaves an empty drawing. Undo and Redo recover either action. Removal clears the old live preview immediately, so particles from a previous shape cannot look like the shape is still present. Click Preview to replay the revised design.</p><p>Adding to a library shape makes the main shape editable. Its opening burst step follows the drawing if it previously used that main shape without a separate snapshot. Other layers and burst steps are managed in their own tabs and can still create stars. Save Shell, then save your project, to keep additions, removals and names.</p><p>Choose <b>Draw Shape</b> in the Workspace card in the tools column. <b>Undo</b> and <b>Redo</b> stay at the top of the tools column. Choose Freehand to draw with the mouse; <b>Auto-smooth freehand</b> is on by default and rounds the stroke when you release the mouse. Set <b>Correction strength</b> higher for softer curves or lower to retain more detail. Freehand ignores grid snap to avoid jagged steps.</p><p>Use <b>Smooth Outline</b> to soften wobble, <b>Round Corners</b> to add editable curved corners without moving the outline points, or <b>Reduce Extra Points</b> to simplify a busy outline. These apply to the shape selected in Shapes in This Design; they keep open endpoints in place. Undo restores the original. Correction applies to outlines, not individual-star placement.</p><p>Curve draws editable Bezier handles. Line, Box, Oval and Star provide precise shapes. Select supports Ctrl selection, box selection, group dragging and arrow-key nudging; Shift makes a larger nudge. Use Shapes in This Design to select or remove shapes; Selected Shape opens, closes or mirrors the selected shape. Selected Stars assigns color and effect to selected points or the current outline.</p><p>Import &amp; Text accepts SVG geometry or up to twelve characters. SVG import reads supported vector outlines and transforms, ignoring scripts and external content. Save Shell writes the working design into your project; save the project to keep it.</p><p>Drawing tools and correction appear near the top of the tools column when Draw Shape is selected. Selected Shape, Selected Stars, and Import &amp; Text expand when needed. Click a row in Shapes in This Design to select it; each row has its own Remove button. Selecting points does not use an Undo step.</p>"}, {"id": "lab-geometry", "category": "Shell Lab", "title": "Control shapes and splitting stars", "summary": "Adjust pattern detail, star placement and smaller bursts.", "tags": "shape geometry petals arms turns rings spacing star points gears hold splitting fragments generations", "related": ["lab-drawing", "lab-sequence", "lab-curves"], "body": "<p>In Shape, enable Customize Star Placement. Points, gear teeth, petals, arms, turns, rings and inner shape size affect matching shape families. Width, height, spacing, thickness, active area and distribution control placement. Physics and Effects remain available.</p><p>With Shape Hold above zero, Formation Time sets how long the drawing takes to form. It stays readable for the artistic hold duration, then releases its stars. Zero artistic hold uses natural motion. Spin, darting, sweeping and cascade add movement. Hold is an artistic option, not a physically exact fireworks model.</p><p>Splitting Stars controls when stars divide, how many new stars they create, their spread, speed and lifetime, and up to three levels of splitting. The particle limit bounds dense effects. Layers &amp; Detail supports twelve layers, solo preview, position offsets and independent movement. Copy Drawing to Layer gives a layer its own shape. Use Main Shell Movement restores inherited settings.</p><p>All library recipes now use a zero-second hold for natural movement. Artistic hold is optional for custom effects.</p><p>Shape-specific controls appear only when the selected shape uses them. Natural spherical shells retain their original placement; outline-only controls remain disabled until you choose an editable shape or edit its outline on the canvas.</p>"}, {"id": "lab-sequence", "category": "Shell Lab", "title": "Arrange timed burst steps", "summary": "Build one shell from up to sixteen timed blooms and accents.", "tags": "burst steps sequence timeline stage delay ignition ring bloom multibreak offsets", "related": ["lab-geometry", "lab-curves", "lab-assets"], "body": "<p>Enable Use Timed Burst Steps to replace fixed repeated breaks. Add, duplicate or remove steps. Each step has a name, shape, start time, size, star count, speed, lifetime, rotation, color, crackle and position. Times begin at the main detonation.</p><p>Drag blocks along the twelve-second strip or enter exact start times. Include the Shell’s Layers lets a step use its layers. Copy Current Shape to Step gives a step its own editable outline snapshot. Copy Current Changes to Step stores its color, movement and splitting programs. The list order helps organize steps; their times control playback. A value of 1 for size, speed or lifetime keeps the main shell value; 0.5 halves it.</p><p>Programmed shells work in show cues and exports. Save Shell, then save the project. Save Burst Steps stores the timing program for reuse in another shell.</p>"}, {"id": "lab-curves", "category": "Shell Lab", "title": "Change color, brightness and movement over time", "summary": "Draw how stars change throughout their lifetime.", "tags": "changes over time curves envelope brightness fade size trail width sparkle gravity drag wind color stops gradient dark interval", "related": ["lab-sequence", "lab-preview", "lab-assets"], "body": "<p>In Changes Over Time, enable Use Timed Color Changes. Add color changes at fractions from 0 to 1 of each star’s life. Smooth Transitions blends them; switching it off gives sharp changes. Black creates dark intervals. Progression follows age, an outward wave or travel around the pattern.</p><p>Enable Use Changes Over Time and choose brightness, star size, trail length, trail width, sparkle, gravity, air drag or wind response. Click to add a point and drag it. Left to right means start to end of life. Height means strength from 0 to 3: 1 keeps the original setting, 2 doubles it. Numeric rows allow exact values. Reset This Change, Gradual Fade and Pulse offer starting shapes.</p><p>Gravity makes stars fall; air drag slows them down. Brightness and trails also retain their natural fade. Shape Hold temporarily overrides movement while the drawing stays readable. These settings do not change the show’s timing.</p>"}, {"id": "lab-preview", "category": "Shell Lab", "title": "Preview shells and compare your changes", "summary": "Play the shell, pause to inspect it, or compare with a saved reference.", "tags": "preview scrub slow frame deterministic seed compare reference A B pause rewind reference before after compare current natural motion artistic hold", "related": ["lab-drawing", "lab-curves", "lab-assets"], "body": "<p>Shell Lab opens on a paused, visible preview so you can inspect the shell. Changing settings pauses playback and automatically refreshes the preview at the current time. If playback has finished, the refresh returns to a useful inspection point. The status below the canvas shows when the preview is updating.</p><p><b>Preview</b> is at the bottom, immediately left of Pause/Resume. It switches from the drawing canvas to the shell view, restarts the shell, and plays it. Pause/Resume controls inspection playback; Next Frame advances one frame, Rewind returns to the start, the slider scrubs silently, and the speed menu slows playback. These inspection controls do not change the finished show.</p><p>The left <b>Compare Changes</b> card is for before-and-after inspection. Click <b>Keep Current for Comparison</b> before editing. Change the working shell, then click <b>Compare Side by Side</b> to see <b>Saved Reference</b> beside <b>Current Design</b>. Both use the same preview time. Saving a reference again replaces it for this session; it does not save or overwrite a project shell.</p><p>Library recipes use natural motion and do not freeze stars in midair. If you deliberately want a stylized suspended shape, Shape includes <b>Artistic shape hold (s)</b>. Keep it at zero for natural fireworks. Preview may temporarily pause for inspection, but that pause is not part of the shell design or export.</p><p>Changing the shape list clears the old preview and pauses inspection. Press Preview to see the revised drawing. An empty custom drawing creates no main-shape stars; separately configured layers or burst steps can still fire.</p><p>Preview and Resume also work in comparison view. Both designs restart together and use the same time. After playback ends, Resume restarts it.</p>"}, {"id": "lab-assets", "category": "Shell Lab", "title": "Save and share designs you can reuse", "summary": "Keep shapes, color changes, burst steps and complete shells.", "tags": "saved designs assets library import export JSON reusable shell shape colors sequence backup", "related": ["lab-drawing", "lab-sequence", "save-project"], "body": "<p>Saved Designs can save a shape, color changes, burst steps or a complete shell. Name a design, then apply it to another working shell. The library belongs to this PC. Save Shell and save the project to carry the design in a portable show file.</p><p>Export Saved Designs writes a JSON library. Import Saved Designs validates that data and applies collection limits; it does not run scripts or launch imported programs. Keep an exported copy when moving to another PC.</p><p>Removing a library design does not remove copies already applied to project shells. Existing projects keep traditional behavior unless the new controls are enabled. Star, Spiral, Lotus and Double Ring catalog recipes use selected new features; eleven designer shapes are also available.</p><p>Save Shell stays visible at the bottom of the tools column. Unsaved Changes appears beside the shell name until you save it. Save Shell applies the design to the project; then use the main Save button to write the project file. Undo remains available after saving a new shell. Search in the far-left library finds shell names and palettes without moving the options column.</p>"}]);
